    "passage": "Wearing flip-flops is comfortable for tourists having fun on the Indonesian island of Bali. However, it could be catastrophic if tourists went back to their hometown with the same footwear.\nIn the early July this year, the Indonesian government [[1]] an outbreak of Foot and Mouth Disease. Then the virus rapidly spread through cattle in the country. In fact, this type of disease basically won’t do harm to human beings, yet it can lead to horrific [[2]] among cloven-hoofed animals, such as cattle, pigs, goats, and camels. [[3]] animals will develop symptoms, including severe lameness, blisters on mouth and feet, and even deaths. Fearing the spread would be more intense, Australian officials have strengthened the biosecurity controls and examined luggage for meat and cheese products. [[4]], they are even contemplating whether travelers who have visited Bali should abandon flip-flops there to avoid bringing the disease home.\nFMD has not been present in Australia for 150 years. No one would like to [[5]] back in the time when Australia was plagued by this disease, which haunted many livestock owners. If the outbreak occurred again, it could make their lifetime of hard work vaporize overnight. That’s why people are trying their best to keep the disease at bay.",
